    "summary": "Epic three‑set marathon with soaring 'He's Gone' → Truckin' → Other One jam",
    "blurb": "The show shines on the massive \"He's Gone → Truckin' → The Other One → Eyes → China Doll\" sequence and a tight first set featuring \"They Love Each Other\" and \"Playing In The Band\". Jerry’s tone is flawless, Phil delivers lush bass wizardry, and Keith adds depth with his piano/organ work throughout. Multiple soundboard sources capture the performance in superb clarity, making this one of ’73’s standout concerts.",
    "review": "On May 26, 1973 the Dead turned Kezar Stadium into a proving ground for their evolving improvisational fire. The first set rolls on smoothly with bright renditions of \"The Promised Land\", \"Deal\" and a particularly resonant \"They Love Each Other\" that showcases Jerry’s flawless tone and Bob’s wailing rhythm chops. By the time they hit the second set, the band is in full groove – “Here Comes Sunshine” opens a sun‑splashed stretch that leads into the legendary China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue, while Keith’s piano adds a warm undercurrent to “Big River”. The third set is where the night reaches mythic status: a seamless \"He's Gone → Truckin' → The Other One → Eyes Of The World → China Doll\" journey that feels both relentless and lyrical, with Phil’s bass soaring through the peaks and Bill’s drumming driving the momentum. Across all recordings the Miller soundboard capture stands out for its crystal‑clear stereo separation (Jerry right, Bob left) and immaculate mix, preserving every nuance of this marathon three‑set masterpiece.",
